export default {"basecamp/ai-era":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two engineers, one assistant","md":"During a Friday incident, a disk filled up on a payment server. Engineer A pasted the alert into an AI chat, got back `sudo rm -rf /var/log/*`, and ran it. The disk recovered. So did the attackers who had been in the system for a week: the logs that would have shown them were gone, and so was the audit trail the company needed for its compliance report.\n\nEngineer B, on another team, used the same assistant differently. She asked it to explain an unfamiliar error, to draft a runbook from her rough notes, and to suggest *where to look*. She read every suggested command, asked \"what does `-f` do here?\", tried it on staging first, and kept a human decision at every step. Her team's time to recover from incidents dropped by a third.\n\nSame tool. The difference was the person's judgment."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What AI changes, and what it does not","md":"AI assistants (chat models, code copilots, and increasingly **agents** that can run tools) are now part of everyday DevOps work. They are good at some things and dangerous at others.\n\n| AI is strong at | AI is weak at |\n|---|---|\n| Explaining commands, errors and concepts | Knowing *your* systems, history and constraints |\n| Drafting scripts, configs, docs and tests | Recent changes, versions and your company's rules |\n| Summarising long logs, threads and diffs | Knowing when it is wrong (it sounds equally sure either way) |\n| Suggesting where to look next | Understanding side effects and blast radius |\n\nThe failure mode has a name: **hallucination**, a confident answer that is simply invented, such as a flag that does not exist or an API removed two versions ago.\n\n```diagram\nai-verify\n```\n\nThe rule for the rest of this course: **the AI drafts, you decide**. Every suggestion goes through the same loop: read it, understand each part, test it somewhere safe, apply it in a small step, and observe the result.\n\nWhat does not change: you still need the fundamentals. You cannot verify a command you do not understand, and you cannot debug at 3 a.m. with an assistant if you cannot tell a good answer from a bad one. That is why this course teaches the fundamentals first and the AI-assisted workflows on top."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The brilliant new intern","md":"Imagine an intern who has read every manual ever written, answers instantly and never gets tired, but joined this morning, has never seen your systems, and sometimes makes things up rather than say \"I don't know\". You would happily ask them for drafts and ideas. You would not hand them the production root password and go to lunch."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"How good teams use AI","md":"- **No secrets in prompts.** No passwords, keys, tokens or customer data in a chat window.\n- **Review AI output like a junior colleague's pull request**: line by line, with tests.\n- **Humans approve actions.** Agents that run commands get the least privilege possible, a dry-run mode and a human approval step for anything risky.\n- **Keep an audit trail** of what AI tools did and who approved it.\n- **Use AI to learn, not to skip learning.** Ask it *why*, not just *what*."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"AI traps","md":"**Copy, paste, enter.** Running a command you cannot explain on a real system.\n\n**Confidence equals correctness.** The tone of an answer tells you nothing about whether it is right.\n\n**Fixing the symptom.** \"Delete the logs\" makes the alert go away; it does not answer why the disk filled up.\n\n**Over-sharing.** Pasting a config file with database passwords into a public AI tool is a security incident."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Your new AI pair","md":"Your laptop's disk is almost full and builds are failing. The terminal has a simulated AI assistant: type `ai \"your question\"` to ask it, and `ai explain \"<command>\"` to have any command broken down with a risk rating.\n\nUse it the way Engineer B would: as a helper whose suggestions you check."}},"basecamp/basecamp-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Your first day at Lumen","md":"It is your first morning on Lumen's platform team. You have barely set up your laptop when the team chat lights up: **\"Checkout feels broken, can someone look?\"** Everyone senior is in a planning meeting.\n\nYou will not fix the problem today. Your job is to **investigate calmly and report clearly**: what is broken, what the evidence says, and what you think the next step is. That is the most valuable thing a new person can do in an incident."},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Base Camp gave you","md":"- **DevOps** is shared ownership of the whole loop, with automation and fast feedback; CALMS and the Three Ways describe it, and the four DORA metrics measure it.\n- Delivery is a **loop** and a **value stream**: most lead time is waiting, so shrink queues with small batches, less work in progress and automation.\n- **AI assistants** draft, explain and summarise; you read, verify, test safely and decide.\n- Your first terminal habits: `pwd`, `ls`, `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `grep`, `wc`, `curl`, and `echo … >> file`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Checkout feels broken","md":"Investigate the report. Lumen's public status API is at `https://status.lumen.example.com/api`, and the team keeps its runbooks somewhere in your home folder. Collect evidence and write a short incident note in `~/incident.md`."}},"basecamp/delivery-loop":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Forty-seven days for one button","md":"Lumen's product manager asked a simple question: \"Why did the *Add to calendar* button take 47 days to reach users? It was two days of coding.\"\n\nThe team traced the button's journey through every system it touched. Coding: 2 days. Waiting for a code review: 6 days. Waiting for the next test cycle: 9 days. Manual testing: 3 days. Waiting for the monthly release window: 22 days. Release and verification: 5 days.\n\nOnly **10 of the 47 days** involved anyone working on it. The rest was **waiting** in queues between teams. Making developers type faster would have changed almost nothing. Shrinking the queues changed everything: smaller pull requests got reviewed the same day, automated tests replaced the test cycle, and continuous delivery removed the release window. The next button took four days."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The loop and the value stream","md":"Software delivery is a loop, not a line. What you learn from running software feeds the next plan.\n\n```diagram\ndevops-loop\n```\n\n| Phase | The question it answers | Typical tools (you will use all of these) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Plan | What should we build next, and why? | Issues, roadmaps |\n| Code | How do we build it together? | Git, pull requests, code review |\n| Build | Can we turn it into something runnable? | Compilers, package managers, Docker |\n| Test | Does it work, and is it safe? | Unit and integration tests, scanners |\n| Release | Is this exact version ready to ship? | Version tags, artifacts, registries |\n| Deploy | How do we put it in front of users safely? | Pipelines, Kubernetes, rollouts |\n| Operate | How do we keep it running? | Linux, systemd, cloud, infrastructure as code |\n| Monitor | Is it healthy? Are users happy? | Metrics, logs, traces, alerts |\n\nA **value stream** is every step from \"someone has an idea\" to \"a user benefits from it\". Two numbers matter:\n\n- **Lead time**: how long the whole journey takes.\n- **Process time**: how much of that someone actually worked on it.\n\nThe ratio between them, the *flow efficiency*, is usually shocking: 10 to 20% is common. The biggest wins come from removing waiting, not from working faster.\n\nThree levers shrink waiting:\n\n1. **Smaller batches**: small changes are reviewed, tested and deployed faster, and are easier to debug.\n2. **Less work in progress**: finishing beats starting. Ten half-done things deliver nothing.\n3. **Automation of handoffs**: a pipeline does not have a queue of tickets."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The coffee shop queue","md":"A coffee shop can have the fastest barista in the city, but if every order first waits at a cashier who takes payments one at a time, then for a manager to approve oat milk, the line still goes out the door. You speed the shop up by removing the waits between people, not by asking the barista to move faster."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Reading the evidence","md":"DevOps work starts with evidence, and evidence usually lives in text files: logs, exports, reports. A handful of commands go a long way:\n\n```term\n$ cat feature-4521.log          # print the whole file\n$ head -n 3 feature-4521.log    # just the first 3 lines\n$ tail -n 2 feature-4521.log    # just the last 2 lines\n$ wc -l feature-4521.log        # count the lines\n$ grep -i wait feature-4521.log # only lines containing \"wait\" (any case)\n$ echo \"note\" >> findings.txt   # append a line to a file\n```\n\nYou will learn these properly in the Linux realm; here they help you investigate."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Making flow visible","md":"Teams that improve delivery make the work visible:\n\n- A board where every item shows its stage, and how long it has waited there.\n- A weekly look at the DORA metrics, not to blame anyone but to spot the biggest queue.\n- **Work-in-progress limits**: \"no more than three items in review at once\" forces the team to finish reviews before starting new work.\n- Retrospectives that ask \"where did this wait?\" rather than \"who was slow?\"."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Flow traps","md":"**Optimising one step.** Making builds two minutes faster does not help if changes wait two weeks for a release window.\n\n**Big batches \"to save time\".** One release with 200 changes saves ceremony and costs you a week of debugging when it breaks.\n\n**Measuring people instead of flow.** Lines of code or tickets closed per person encourage the wrong behaviour. Measure the system."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Follow the button","md":"Lumen's tracking system exported the journey of feature **#4521** (the *Add to calendar* button) as a log file in your home folder. Investigate where the time went, and write your findings to a file."}},"basecamp/what-is-devops":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two releases a year","md":"In 2019, Lumen Tickets shipped software twice a year. Developers spent five months writing features, then handed a 400 MB zip file to the operations team with a 30-page installation guide. Release weekend meant pizza, panic and a 2 a.m. call: the new version needed a library the servers did not have, and nobody on the ops side knew which feature was causing the database to melt.\n\nDevelopers were rewarded for shipping features. Operations was rewarded for stability. So developers pushed for change and operations pushed back against it. Both were doing their jobs, and the company lost either way.\n\nFive years later, the same company deploys about 30 times a day. Each change is small, tested automatically, rolled out gradually and watched closely. When something breaks, the developer who wrote it is paged, has a dashboard showing what changed, and can roll back in one command. Outages still happen, but they are smaller, shorter and less frightening.\n\nThey did not just buy new tools. They changed **who owns what**, **how work flows**, and **how fast they learn**. That change is what people mean by **DevOps**."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What DevOps is (and is not)","md":"**DevOps** is a way of building and running software in which the same team owns the whole journey from idea to production, and uses automation and fast feedback to make that journey safe, quick and repeatable.\n\n```diagram\nwall-of-confusion\n```\n\nA useful checklist is **CALMS**:\n\n| Pillar | Means | Looks like |\n|---|---|---|\n| **C**ulture | Shared ownership, no blame | \"You build it, you run it\"; blameless postmortems |\n| **A**utomation | Machines do the repetitive, error-prone steps | Pipelines, infrastructure as code, auto-rollbacks |\n| **L**ean | Small batches, limit waiting, remove waste | Small pull requests, deploys several times a day |\n| **M**easurement | Decide with data | Delivery metrics, dashboards, service-level objectives |\n| **S**haring | Knowledge flows between people and teams | Runbooks, docs, pairing, open postmortems |\n\nThe **Three Ways** describe the direction of travel:\n\n1. **Flow**: make work move quickly and smoothly from idea to user (small batches, automation, fewer handoffs).\n2. **Feedback**: see problems quickly and close to where they started (tests, monitoring, alerts that reach the right people).\n3. **Continuous learning**: experiment, learn from failures, and get better every week.\n\nAnd four numbers, the **DORA metrics**, tell you how well it works:\n\n- **Deployment frequency**: how often you ship.\n- **Lead time for changes**: from commit to running in production.\n- **Change failure rate**: what share of deploys cause a problem.\n- **Time to restore service**: how fast you recover when they do.\n\nElite teams score well on all four at once: speed and stability are not a trade-off."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The pit crew","md":"A Formula 1 pit stop takes about two seconds. Twenty people change four tyres, adjust the wing and send the car out. Nobody hands a form to another department; every person knows the whole sequence, the tools are laid out in advance, every move is rehearsed, and they review the footage afterwards to shave off another tenth.\n\nNow imagine the tyres were changed by a separate company that only hears about the stop when the car arrives, with no rehearsal and a 30-page manual. That is the old dev-and-ops split. DevOps is building the pit crew."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Who does DevOps?","md":"Job titles vary, and they cause confusion:\n\n- **Developers** write features and also tests, deployment configuration and dashboards for what they ship.\n- **Site Reliability Engineers (SRE)** treat operations as a software problem: they set reliability targets, automate toil and lead incident response.\n- **Platform engineers** build the internal \"paved road\": pipelines, templates and self-service tools so product teams can ship safely without being experts in everything.\n- **\"DevOps engineer\"** usually means someone who builds and runs that automation. Useful, as long as it does not become a new wall between dev and ops.\n\nWhatever the title, the skills overlap: Linux, networking, scripting, CI/CD, containers, cloud, observability, security and, increasingly, working well with AI tools. That is exactly the path ahead of you."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Common misunderstandings","md":"**\"DevOps is a tool.\"** Buying Kubernetes does not make you DevOps, just as buying running shoes does not make you a runner.\n\n**\"DevOps means developers do everything alone.\"** It means shared ownership with good tooling, not dumping ops work on developers without support.\n\n**\"DevOps is a team in the middle.\"** A separate \"DevOps team\" that receives tickets from dev and hands work to ops recreates the wall with a new name.\n\n**\"Moving fast means breaking things.\"** Small, automated, observable changes are *safer* than big, rare ones."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"First contact","md":"Welcome to **Lumen Tickets**. This is your laptop: a Linux machine with a terminal. Everything in the course happens in terminals like this one, connected to simulated servers, pipelines and clusters.\n\nYou do not need to know any commands yet. Each task tells you what to type, and the **Hint** and **Show a solution** buttons help when you are stuck. Watch the panels on the right change as you work."}}};
